        "subject": "3 things that separate good training from great training",
        "emailFormat": "html",
        "html": "=<!DOCTYPE html><html><body style=\"font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;background:#0b0f0c;color:#d4ddd6;max-width:600px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px\"><h2 style=\"color:#7caf58\">3 things that separate good training from great training</h2><ol><li style=\"margin-bottom:12px\"><strong>RIR is not optional.</strong> Every primary lift in your program has a 1–3 rep-in-reserve target. Going to failure on strength work destroys your recovery capacity and compounds fatigue over weeks.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:12px\"><strong>CNS load matters more than volume.</strong> High-CNS sessions (heavy compound lifts, jumps, power work) are capped at 2 per week. Your program already accounts for this — follow the block order.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:12px\"><strong>Your fatigue score drives everything.</strong> When you return for your next assessment, accurate sleep, soreness, energy, and stress inputs produce the most precise program. Don't round up.</li></ol><p>Your program: <a href=\"https://deusperformance.com/program/{{ $json.run_id }}\" style=\"color:#7caf58\">deusperformance.com/program/{{ $json.run_id }}</a></p><p style=\"color:#6b7a6e;font-size:12px\">Deus Performance · Riz Management LLC</p></body></html>",
        "options": {}
      },
